1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,120
And now, for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,380 --> 00:00:11,980
So these two bear biologists are headed off into the woods.

3
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:13,700
Wait a minute.

4
00:00:14,020 --> 00:00:16,560
They're not bear biologists.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,440
They're wearing clothing, but they're studying bears.

6
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:21,160
You got it?

7
00:00:22,460 --> 00:00:22,700
Yeah.

8
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:28,820
So they're headed way off into the backcountry to do a field study on some bears,

9
00:00:28,820 --> 00:00:31,580
put on their backpacks, hike in.

10
00:00:31,660 --> 00:00:34,060
They get about five miles in, and they get to a trail sign,

11
00:00:34,120 --> 00:00:36,580
and the sign says, Bear Left.

12
00:00:37,860 --> 00:00:39,320
So they said, well, what the heck?

13
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:40,480
We might as well go home.

14
00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:45,019
Hey, it's podcast number 33.

15
00:00:45,019 --> 00:00:45,140
Hey!

16
00:00:58,820 --> 00:01:17,280
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

17
00:01:17,539 --> 00:01:18,760
This is Clark Green.

18
00:01:18,760 --> 00:01:26,400
In our 33rd podcast, we're going to begin with Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

19
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:28,220
We're going to talk a little bit about den chiefs,

20
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:32,820
and featuring an interview I did with one of the scouts in our troop who's a den chief.

21
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:38,660
Then the return of this has got to be true because nobody could make this up,

22
00:01:38,720 --> 00:01:41,060
a story from summer camp.

23
00:01:41,060 --> 00:01:45,760
And we're going to finish with answering a couple of emails.

24
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:47,320
That's a lot to do.

25
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:48,880
Let's get started, shall we?

26
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:53,440
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

27
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:04,520
So school goes back in session, and scouting gets ready to begin again.

28
00:02:05,700 --> 00:02:10,160
Troops and packs are beginning to meet, and they're starting to sort things out,

29
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:13,240
and they've settled their schedule, and they're ready to be active again.

30
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:14,420
Let me ask you this.

31
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:18,720
How many den chiefs are part of your troop leadership?

32
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:20,440
Den chiefs.

33
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:23,220
Now, you should be familiar with the role of den chief.

34
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:27,760
This is a scout who's going to go and assist a Cub Scout den.

35
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:30,980
And this can be a den of any description.

36
00:02:31,140 --> 00:02:34,620
It can be all the way from Tiger Cubs through Weeblos 2.

37
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:39,260
A den chief is a wonderful job for a scout.

38
00:02:39,260 --> 00:02:41,519
It's a great thing for them to do.

39
00:02:41,940 --> 00:02:43,799
It's a school of leadership for them.

40
00:02:44,239 --> 00:02:46,880
It really can be a very big confidence builder.

41
00:02:47,399 --> 00:02:53,600
And it really, it not only does that, but it assists the Cubs and the den leader.

42
00:02:54,120 --> 00:03:00,239
And it also helps to cement a relationship with those Cubs when it's time for them to choose a troop

43
00:03:00,239 --> 00:03:03,120
to graduate into after Weeblos 2.

44
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:08,720
So, den chiefs are a very important aspect of scouting,

45
00:03:09,060 --> 00:03:12,280
and it needs to be guys who know what they're about

46
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:17,620
and who are able to really bring something to the table as a den chief.

47
00:03:17,620 --> 00:03:24,280
We've always used the position of den chief as kind of an entry-level leadership position

48
00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:27,440
because we know it's going to be supervised pretty closely by an adult,

49
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:32,120
that it's going to be working with Cubs who are,

50
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:35,780
well, I know they're all over the map behavior-wise,

51
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:39,680
but usually they show a great deal of deference and respect to an older scout.

52
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:42,320
And it works very well.

53
00:03:42,320 --> 00:03:48,079
Now, I had the opportunity earlier this year to sit down and talk with one of my scouts

54
00:03:48,079 --> 00:03:51,100
who works as a den chief and just asked him a few questions about it,

55
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:52,560
and I thought it might be interesting for you.

56
00:03:52,560 --> 00:03:54,680
The job is what I thought it was going to be.

57
00:03:54,799 --> 00:03:59,280
It was going to be helping out with the running of a Cub Scout den,

58
00:03:59,380 --> 00:04:00,940
and that's exactly what it is.

59
00:04:01,140 --> 00:04:04,359
I come, I help out with whatever they need me to do.

60
00:04:05,299 --> 00:04:06,600
And a positive experience for you?

61
00:04:06,959 --> 00:04:11,260
Yeah, because I get to, I mean, I get to go back to Cub Scouts,

62
00:04:11,260 --> 00:04:13,299
which is kind of like a neat time.

63
00:04:13,540 --> 00:04:16,980
And also, I get to help out.

64
00:04:17,079 --> 00:04:20,019
I mean, that's always a good feeling, you know?

65
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:20,839
Yeah.

66
00:04:21,320 --> 00:04:22,380
Has it been aggravating enough?

67
00:04:23,039 --> 00:04:24,440
I wouldn't say so.

68
00:04:26,280 --> 00:04:27,159
No, not at all.

69
00:04:27,260 --> 00:04:30,020
It hasn't really infringed on my schedule in any way.

70
00:04:30,780 --> 00:04:36,140
What would be, like, the optimum age, in your humble opinion, age for a dent chief?

71
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:37,260
The optimum age?

72
00:04:38,700 --> 00:04:39,760
14 or 15.

73
00:04:40,419 --> 00:04:42,060
You think that's the youngest you would go?

74
00:04:42,380 --> 00:04:47,419
That's, no, I think that's about, like, the center, the median age, I guess,

75
00:04:47,539 --> 00:04:49,020
for somebody who would be doing this.

76
00:04:49,140 --> 00:04:50,520
So you would go a little bit younger?

77
00:04:50,520 --> 00:04:50,599
Sure.

78
00:04:51,919 --> 00:04:56,599
I would be, I would think that somebody who is 13 could easily do this.

79
00:04:56,880 --> 00:04:57,140
Okay.

80
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:04,380
What would be the two or three things you would tell somebody who is going to be a dent chief?

81
00:05:05,479 --> 00:05:10,799
Well, first they have to be good with, like, you know, little kids and not get completely annoyed.

82
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:19,340
Or, like, you know, go, like, completely around the bend when they have to come in contact with them for an extended period of time.

83
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:26,100
But, I mean, other than that, it's not, it's certainly not a difficult job.

84
00:05:26,840 --> 00:05:37,120
It's, um, it's a responsibility, but it's not something that's going to, like, completely throw a wrench into your schedule.

85
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:45,520
Well, that should give you a pretty good idea of a few reflections from a scout who has served as a dent chief and the kind of things he experienced.

86
00:05:46,340 --> 00:05:51,320
This particular scout went on to become a senior patrol leader.

87
00:05:52,180 --> 00:05:59,220
He had served as a patrol leader prior to his dent chief experience, which sounds a little backwards,

88
00:05:59,560 --> 00:06:06,280
because usually you would think, like I said earlier, a dent chief is kind of an entry-level leadership position,

89
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:11,479
and then that moves on into patrol leader and then one of the other more senior leadership positions in the troop.

90
00:06:12,179 --> 00:06:21,119
I had this wild idea, thinking that my 15-, 16-year-old scouts, who had done a lot of leadership and things like that

91
00:06:21,119 --> 00:06:28,580
and didn't have as active a role in the, you know, senior leadership of the troop, might be interested.

92
00:06:28,580 --> 00:06:36,359
We offered them the idea of going and helping out with Cub Scout dens, and several of them caught on and really enjoyed themselves.

93
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:44,880
They went back having, you know, a little more maturity and a little more knowledge, and they were really able to help out,

94
00:06:45,280 --> 00:06:48,820
and the den leaders really appreciated it as well.

95
00:06:48,820 --> 00:06:50,540
So there you go.

96
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:55,260
There's just a quick little reflection on what dent chiefs are and what they do,

97
00:06:55,580 --> 00:07:00,020
and an encouragement to you to get a good dent chief program running in your troop as soon as you can.

98
00:07:01,159 --> 00:07:08,920
This has to be the truth, folks, because there is no way anyone could make this up.

99
00:07:08,920 --> 00:07:36,840
So finally, finally, the Sunday that we head off for our week at summer camp rolls around.

100
00:07:36,840 --> 00:07:44,799
And I've got all my stuff, and the scouts are on their way, and we head on down.

101
00:07:44,900 --> 00:07:51,719
We get moved into our campsite, and we're getting settled, and parents have dropped the scouts off.

102
00:07:52,599 --> 00:07:58,239
Myself and two other adult leaders are there with about 24, 25 boys.

103
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,099
You know, 24, 25.

104
00:08:02,900 --> 00:08:04,179
Who counts, right?

105
00:08:04,179 --> 00:08:06,239
I mean, they move around so much.

106
00:08:06,240 --> 00:08:07,760
When can you ever count them?

107
00:08:20,340 --> 00:08:28,220
But anyway, we're down there, and, you know, as the guys are getting ready to head out for the health check

108
00:08:28,220 --> 00:08:32,299
and the swim test and things like that, my cell phone rings.

109
00:08:32,299 --> 00:08:35,120
You heard me.

110
00:08:35,260 --> 00:08:38,599
My cell phone rings right in the campsite.

111
00:08:40,919 --> 00:08:41,359
Yeah.

112
00:08:42,520 --> 00:08:45,459
I guess they put a new tower up somewhere.

113
00:08:45,579 --> 00:08:48,099
Now, we're in a reasonably built-up area.

114
00:08:49,300 --> 00:08:57,359
Here in the mid-Atlantic states, you know, there's not too many places I guess you can go without your cell phone ringing.

115
00:08:57,360 --> 00:08:57,860
Ugh.

116
00:09:09,680 --> 00:09:18,340
So anyway, I answer the cell phone, and I get a message from one of the parents who's just dropped one of the boys off that there's a big thunderstorm heading our way.

117
00:09:18,340 --> 00:09:22,680
And, yeah, you know, the thunderstorm comes and passes through a little later on that afternoon.

118
00:09:23,760 --> 00:09:35,400
And then we've been off to retreat and dinner and to a big campfire, and we're sitting around our picnic table in the evening.

119
00:09:35,400 --> 00:09:39,000
And the boys are headed off to bed.

120
00:09:39,500 --> 00:09:41,000
Taps is about to blow soon.

121
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:48,500
One of the guys that's with me, one of the adults that's down there with me, assistant scoutmaster, says, you know, I think I'll give my wife a call.

122
00:09:49,400 --> 00:09:52,260
And I said, oh, yeah, fine.

123
00:09:52,260 --> 00:10:01,620
So he takes a step or two away from the picnic table, gets the phone, dials his home number.

124
00:10:13,100 --> 00:10:15,980
And we only heard one side of the conversation, obviously.

125
00:10:17,100 --> 00:10:18,800
He said, yeah, hi, we're here.

126
00:10:19,080 --> 00:10:20,680
Oh, yes, he's doing fine.

127
00:10:20,680 --> 00:10:21,839
Yeah, I'm fine.

128
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:25,560
No, the storm didn't hit us too bad or anything like that.

129
00:10:26,640 --> 00:10:27,900
And then he says,

130
00:10:28,079 --> 00:10:33,140
then he says,

131
00:10:33,439 --> 00:10:34,839
yeah, honey, it's amazing.

132
00:10:34,939 --> 00:10:38,439
The cell phones work right here in the campsite.

133
00:10:38,440 --> 00:10:58,340
And just as he gets the last part of the word campsite out of his mouth, his face falls and a look of horror spreads across his face.

134
00:10:58,400 --> 00:11:00,840
And he looks at the two of us sitting at the picnic table.

135
00:11:00,840 --> 00:11:04,320
And of course, we too are horrified.

136
00:11:05,360 --> 00:11:10,660
And those words are words that we could never get back.

137
00:11:12,020 --> 00:11:16,360
So we just had to turn the cell phones off for the rest of the week.

138
00:11:17,040 --> 00:11:17,940
Oh, what a pity.

139
00:11:17,940 --> 00:11:18,760
What a shame.

140
00:11:31,160 --> 00:11:34,140
Yeah, honey, the cell phones work right here in the campsite.

141
00:11:34,280 --> 00:11:36,800
Oh, I'll never forget that look.

142
00:11:38,240 --> 00:11:39,900
Yeah, true story.

143
00:11:39,900 --> 00:12:06,340
He's my favorite all time boy scout.

144
00:12:06,340 --> 00:12:13,220
He's my favorite all time boy scout.

145
00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:20,140
You're my favorite all time boy scout.

146
00:12:20,460 --> 00:12:23,540
You're my favorite all time boy scout.

147
00:12:23,840 --> 00:12:26,920
You were always on the beat boy, beat boy.

148
00:12:27,080 --> 00:12:30,400
Yeah, you were always on the beat boy, beat boy.

149
00:12:30,740 --> 00:12:33,840
I'm hanging in the street, boy, street boy.

150
00:12:33,840 --> 00:12:37,240
You were always on the beat boy, beat boy.

151
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:40,680
Yeah, you're my favorite all time boy scout.

152
00:12:40,960 --> 00:12:44,120
You're my favorite all time boy scout.

153
00:12:44,900 --> 00:12:48,360
He's my favorite all time boy scout.

154
00:12:49,220 --> 00:12:51,400
Write me a letter.

155
00:12:52,040 --> 00:12:53,620
Send it by name.

156
00:12:55,220 --> 00:12:56,760
Email, that is, folks.

157
00:12:57,300 --> 00:13:00,340
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

158
00:13:00,340 --> 00:13:12,120
Here's an email I received from Scoutmaster Mike in Kentucky.

159
00:13:12,660 --> 00:13:14,840
He says, I've really been appreciating your podcast.

160
00:13:15,100 --> 00:13:17,360
I'm a new Scoutmaster and have an important question.

161
00:13:18,220 --> 00:13:20,900
Our troops' older Scouts are down quite a bit in numbers,

162
00:13:21,220 --> 00:13:24,100
and that means we have a whole bunch of new Scouts

163
00:13:24,100 --> 00:13:25,420
and just a few older Scouts.

164
00:13:25,420 --> 00:13:29,459
With this, our patrol method utilization has been lacking.

165
00:13:30,240 --> 00:13:32,479
So what's the number one thing, in your opinion,

166
00:13:32,560 --> 00:13:34,540
you can do to jumpstart the patrol method?

167
00:13:35,680 --> 00:13:39,439
Well, Mike, I actually get this question quite a bit.

168
00:13:40,920 --> 00:13:43,520
People listen to the podcast,

169
00:13:43,780 --> 00:13:46,099
or they just kind of observe what's going on within the troop,

170
00:13:46,160 --> 00:13:47,099
and they say, yeah, you know,

171
00:13:47,140 --> 00:13:49,079
we don't really have much of a patrol method going.

172
00:13:49,080 --> 00:13:54,020
You know, my answer to those questions usually are

173
00:13:54,020 --> 00:13:56,080
just start using patrols.

174
00:13:57,600 --> 00:14:00,820
It's, honestly, it's really kind of that simple.

175
00:14:01,220 --> 00:14:04,320
Don't worry about the boys.

176
00:14:04,540 --> 00:14:05,380
They will get it.

177
00:14:05,580 --> 00:14:06,800
They will get it very quickly.

178
00:14:07,680 --> 00:14:09,860
You know, just get to the next troop meeting

179
00:14:09,860 --> 00:14:14,540
and have the guys divide themselves into patrols,

180
00:14:14,840 --> 00:14:16,640
have them elect a patrol leader,

181
00:14:17,060 --> 00:14:18,820
have them elect a senior patrol leader,

182
00:14:19,080 --> 00:14:20,260
and let them at it.

183
00:14:21,420 --> 00:14:22,800
That's really what it takes.

184
00:14:23,680 --> 00:14:25,400
Boys understand this intuitively.

185
00:14:25,860 --> 00:14:29,000
They form groups and gangs and cliques

186
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:30,600
and things like that all the time.

187
00:14:31,060 --> 00:14:33,560
So they understand exactly what a patrol is.

188
00:14:33,920 --> 00:14:36,320
They might not call it a patrol outside of scouting,

189
00:14:36,560 --> 00:14:38,340
but they understand exactly what it is.

190
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:40,700
They understand being a member of a team.

191
00:14:40,820 --> 00:14:41,760
They understand leadership.

192
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:43,380
They understand how to do things.

193
00:14:44,060 --> 00:14:45,700
Now, you'll need to train your leaders,

194
00:14:45,700 --> 00:14:48,800
but that training doesn't need to be, you know,

195
00:14:49,140 --> 00:14:51,780
three weekends, taking them away

196
00:14:51,780 --> 00:14:54,460
and showing them a lot to PowerPoint slides.

197
00:14:54,620 --> 00:14:56,800
They can be trained in five-minute increments

198
00:14:56,800 --> 00:14:58,780
just before it's time for them to lead.

199
00:14:59,520 --> 00:15:02,800
It really is, really is kind of that simple.

200
00:15:02,800 --> 00:15:05,839
If scouts haven't been a part of a real patrol,

201
00:15:06,180 --> 00:15:07,479
they'll have some things to learn,

202
00:15:07,760 --> 00:15:09,319
but they learn so very quickly.

203
00:15:10,219 --> 00:15:11,640
Once you have patrol leaders leading,

204
00:15:11,939 --> 00:15:14,260
you'll have to keep the adults out of the way

205
00:15:14,260 --> 00:15:16,780
and let the boys take care of things,

206
00:15:17,099 --> 00:15:18,040
let them do things.

207
00:15:18,760 --> 00:15:20,780
Now, it will take some time

208
00:15:20,780 --> 00:15:23,300
to make things happen

209
00:15:23,300 --> 00:15:25,640
to where they're really going

210
00:15:25,640 --> 00:15:27,939
and really moving on and things like that,

211
00:15:27,940 --> 00:15:31,760
but the group of scouts that starts up

212
00:15:31,760 --> 00:15:33,140
in this type of way

213
00:15:33,140 --> 00:15:36,000
who form patrols and elect their leadership

214
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:36,820
and everything like that,

215
00:15:36,860 --> 00:15:38,520
they're going to be very energized

216
00:15:38,520 --> 00:15:41,100
and very excited about the process.

217
00:15:41,740 --> 00:15:43,660
A lot of times, the biggest obstacle

218
00:15:43,660 --> 00:15:46,620
of making the patrol system happen

219
00:15:46,620 --> 00:15:51,620
is our impatience and reluctance and fear as adults,

220
00:15:51,860 --> 00:15:54,160
and those obstacles really don't go away.

221
00:15:54,240 --> 00:15:55,560
We just learn how to ignore them.

222
00:15:55,560 --> 00:15:59,239
So that's my advice to anybody

223
00:15:59,239 --> 00:16:04,719
who is in this kind of patrol system conundrum.

224
00:16:05,859 --> 00:16:06,900
Just get it rolling.

225
00:16:07,439 --> 00:16:09,060
The boys know how to take care of it.

226
00:16:09,339 --> 00:16:10,520
They know real well,

227
00:16:10,719 --> 00:16:12,839
and you'll be surprised how good they are at it.

228
00:16:14,359 --> 00:16:15,540
Here's another email

229
00:16:15,540 --> 00:16:17,839
that I received

230
00:16:17,839 --> 00:16:19,780
with another pretty common question, actually,

231
00:16:20,199 --> 00:16:22,099
and this email comes to me

232
00:16:22,099 --> 00:16:23,739
from Jerry in Texas.

233
00:16:23,740 --> 00:16:25,780
Here's what Jerry had to say.

234
00:16:25,900 --> 00:16:25,980
He says,

235
00:16:26,060 --> 00:16:26,860
First, let me thank you

236
00:16:26,860 --> 00:16:27,820
for the many hours of work

237
00:16:27,820 --> 00:16:28,980
that you put into the podcast.

238
00:16:29,740 --> 00:16:31,060
Your candor, wisdom, and humor

239
00:16:31,060 --> 00:16:31,840
is remarkable

240
00:16:31,840 --> 00:16:33,620
and as a scoutmaster, very relevant.

241
00:16:34,160 --> 00:16:34,940
Well, thanks, Jerry.

242
00:16:35,040 --> 00:16:36,640
It's always good to hear good things.

243
00:16:37,460 --> 00:16:39,420
I'm glad you find the podcast useful.

244
00:16:40,620 --> 00:16:42,620
I do have a question like your input.

